{
  "title": "Mi {pp:sono svegliata} presto: {ip:lavoro} qui da un anno",
  "translation": "I woke up early: I've been working here for a year",
  "topics": "Reflexive verbs in the past, expressing duration and frequency with da, per, fino a",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      {
        "h": "Reflexive verbs in the past"
      },
      "Reflexive verbs always take *essere* in the [pp:passato prossimo], so the participle agrees with the subject. The pronoun stays in front of the auxiliary.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "",
            "A man",
            "A woman"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "io",
              "mi {pp:sono svegliat**o**}",
              "mi {pp:sono svegliat**a**}"
            ],
            [
              "tu",
              "ti {pp:sei svegliat**o**}",
              "ti {pp:sei svegliat**a**}"
            ],
            [
              "lui / lei",
              "si {pp:è svegliat**o**}",
              "si {pp:è svegliat**a**}"
            ],
            [
              "noi",
              "ci {pp:siamo svegliat**i**}",
              "ci {pp:siamo svegliat**e**}"
            ],
            [
              "voi",
              "vi {pp:siete svegliat**i**}",
              "vi {pp:siete svegliat**e**}"
            ],
            [
              "loro",
              "si {pp:sono svegliat**i**}",
              "si {pp:sono svegliat**e**}"
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "*Non* comes before the pronoun: *Non mi {pp:sono svegliata} presto*.",
      "Some verbs are reflexive in Italian only: *divertirsi* (to have fun), *riposarsi* (to rest), *ricordarsi* (to remember), *arrabbiarsi* (to get angry), *sentirsi* (to feel).",
      {
        "h": "With a modal verb or an infinitive"
      },
      "The pronoun attaches to the infinitive and matches the person: *{ip:Devo} alzarmi presto. {ip:Vuoi} riposarti?* It can also go before the modal: *Mi {ip:devo} alzare presto.*",
      {
        "h": "Da or per?"
      },
      "Ask whether the action is still going on. If it is, Italian uses the present with *da*, unlike English: *{ip:Studio} italiano da un anno* means that you started a year ago and you are still studying. If it is over, use the [pp:passato prossimo] with *per*.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Word",
            "Use",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "da",
              "still going on, since",
              "{ip:Lavoro} qui da un anno. {ip:Abito} qui dal 2020."
            ],
            [
              "per",
              "finished block of time",
              "{pp:Ho lavorato} lì per un anno."
            ],
            [
              "fino a",
              "until",
              "{ip:Resto} fino a domenica."
            ],
            [
              "da… a",
              "from… to",
              "{ip:Lavoro} dalle nove alle cinque."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "To ask: *Da quanto tempo {ip:studi} italiano?* (How long have you been studying Italian?)",
      {
        "h": "How often"
      },
      "*Ogni giorno* (every day), *una volta alla settimana* (once a week), *due volte al mese* (twice a month), *tutti i giorni* (every day), *di rado* (rarely)."
    ]
  },
